{"metadata":{"citation_count_without_self_citations":93,"publication_info":[{"year":1971,"page_end":"747","page_start":"739","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.A","journal_volume":"4"}],"citation_count":95,"dois":[{"value":"10.1103/PhysRevA.4.739"}],"titles":[{"title":"Brownian Motion of a Quantum Oscillator"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"478b61a2-6554-49e8-96e6-54fa4688647b","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1951516"},"full_name":"Agarwal, G.S.","affiliations":[{"value":"Rochester U.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903164"}}],"signature_block":"AGARWALg"}],"curated":true,"texkeys":["Agarwal:1971zz"],"citeable":true,"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"The theory of Brownian motion of a quantum oscillator is developed. The Brownian motion is described by a model Hamiltonian which is taken to be the one describing the interaction between this oscillator and a reservoir. Use is made of the master equation recently derived by the author, to obtain the equation of motion for the various reduced phase-space distribution functions that are obtained by mapping the density operator onto c-number functions. The equations of motion for the reduced phase-space distribution functions are found to be of the Fokker-Planck type. On transforming the Fokker-Planck equation to real variables, it is found to have the same form as the Fokker-Planck equation obtained by Wang and Uhlenbeck to describe the Brownian motion of a classical oscillator. The Fokker-Planck equation is solved for the conditional probability (Green's function) which is found to be in the form of a two-dimensional Gaussian distribution. This solution is then used to obtain various time-dependent quantum statistical properties of the oscillator. Next, the entropy for a quantum oscillator undergoing Brownian motion is calculated and we show that this system approaches equilibrium as t→∞. Finally we show that in the weak-coupling limit the Fokker-Planck equation reduces to the one obtained by making the usual rotating-wave approximation.","source":"APS"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/45521"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"284","page_start":"284","journal_title":"Physica","journal_volume":"7"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/46675"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"823","page_start":"823","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.","journal_volume":"36"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/4793"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"1","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Rev.Mod.Phys.","journal_volume":"15"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"323","page_start":"323","journal_title":"Rev.Mod.Phys.","journal_volume":"17"}}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"27","page_start":"27","journal_title":"Physica","journal_volume":"32"}}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"56","page_start":"56","journal_title":"Physica","journal_volume":"32"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/9385"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"407","page_start":"407","journal_title":"J.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"2"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"180","page_start":"180","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett.","journal_volume":"21"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/68375"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"2161","page_start":"2161","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"2"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/54300"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"350","page_start":"350","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.","journal_volume":"172"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/55568"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"2025","page_st